WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.220
Star Wars has been around for decades,&nbsp;
so it's a sad but inevitable reality that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.360
many of the actors who have appeared in that&nbsp;
galaxy far, far away are no longer with us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:13.260
Here are a few faces from the franchise&nbsp;
you may not have realized passed away.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.540
Admiral Ackbar had a pretty small role in&nbsp;
the original Star Wars trilogy — in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:22.680
he only appeared briefly in Return of the Jedi.&nbsp;
Even still, the character's appearance made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.503
him a firm fan favorite, as did the iconic line&nbsp;
that has now passed on into pop culture legend.

00:00:27.503 --> 00:00:32.357
"It's a trap!"
"Wow, Admiral Ackbar!"

00:00:32.357 --> 00:00:36.420
Naturally, then, fans of Star Wars mourned the&nbsp;
unfortunate passing of Ackbar's voice actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:42.480
Erik Bauersfeld, who died in 2016 at the age of&nbsp;
93. Interestingly, Bauersfeld once argued that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.680
he thought of his movie roles as a secondary&nbsp;
career that he had accidentally stumbled into,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:50.580
as opposed to his more solid work&nbsp;
as a radio producer. He once wrote:

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:54.300
"The voice work I did in movies was&nbsp;
accidental. I was working with Randy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:58.620
Thom on radio dramas at his technical&nbsp;
quarters at Lucasfilm. One day Ben Burtt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:02.700
sound designer for Star Wars, came by and asked&nbsp;
if I would audition for a voice in the movie."

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:08.100
Bauersfeld also voiced Bib Fortuna, one of Jabba&nbsp;
the Hutt's associates, and he returned to the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:12.360
of Admiral Ackbar in The Force Awakens, which&nbsp;
released just months before he passed away.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:18.060
As he dies in the opening hour of the film,&nbsp;
Uncle Owen can be considered a pretty minor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:22.620
Star Wars character in the grand scheme of things&nbsp;
— but his death is the primary call to arms that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:27.360
sets Luke Skywalker off on his hero's journey.&nbsp;
So in a weird way, Owen is actually one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:32.040
movie's most important characters — making actor&nbsp;
Phil Brown one of the story's pivotal players.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:38.400
Sadly, Brown passed away in 2006 at the age of 89.&nbsp;
Even though he was well-known when Star Wars was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.620
made, he'd faced plenty of hardships as an actor.&nbsp;
In the 1950s, Brown was caught up in Senator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:48.600
Joseph McCarthy's communist trials, and was&nbsp;
actually blacklisted because he refused to comply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.460
with the House Un-American Activities Committee's&nbsp;
demands to name communists in Hollywood. He denied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:57.420
being a communist himself, but was still&nbsp;
forced to move to London to continue acting.

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:01.680
As it happened, George Lucas was filming&nbsp;
in London at the same time and needed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.680 --> 00:02:05.820
local actor with an American accent — so&nbsp;
he brought Brown on board. Not long after,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.820 --> 00:02:10.140
Brown was with Lucas in Tunisia, filming&nbsp;
A New Hope's early scenes on Tatooine.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:17.340
A powerhouse English actress, Shelagh Fraser died&nbsp;
in 2000 at the age of 79. She had played a small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.860
but memorable role in A New Hope, as Luke's aunt&nbsp;
Beru. The character dies early on in the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:26.760
so Fraser was unable to contribute a&nbsp;
lot to the film, but just like Lars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:31.020
Beru's death spurs Luke into action, driving&nbsp;
his character arc through much of the trilogy.

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:35.400
And even though her role in Star Wars is small,&nbsp;
some would argue that she's indirectly responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.220
for Luke becoming the hero he is. At a New York&nbsp;
Comic-Con panel, author Meg Cabot argued that very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:45.660
case, suggesting that Aunt Beru's kindness and&nbsp;
caring for Luke helped him overcome the Dark Side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.160
and defeat the Empire. It also helped him find&nbsp;
the compassion to look for the good left in Darth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.200
Vader, without whom he never would have stopped&nbsp;
Emperor Palpatine. So yeah, nice work, Aunt Beru.

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:59.880
Drewe Henley appeared in the Star Wars&nbsp;
franchise as Red Leader, the pilot who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:04.140
led Luke's squadron in the strike against the&nbsp;
Death Star at the climax of A New Hope. Sadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:08.760
he was accidentally credited as "Drewe Hemley" at&nbsp;
the end of the film, but hey, he at least managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.960
to squeeze in a post-mortem appearance in Rogue&nbsp;
One, thanks to the power of archive footage.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.940
In the first film, Red Leader actually nearly&nbsp;
destroys the Death Star. He launches his torpedoes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:22.500
at the exhaust port, but his aim is slightly off.&nbsp;
Not long after his miss, his ship is destroyed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:26.340
and the poor guy is subsequently killed.&nbsp;
Henley was a well-respected actor when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:30.240
was cast in A New Hope, but was forced to&nbsp;
retire not long after the film was released&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.260
due to his struggles with bipolar disorder.&nbsp;
After working through his mental illness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:40.560
he settled down to run a bed and breakfast with&nbsp;
his wife. Henley died in 2016 at the age of 75.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:45.660
Anakin Skywalker has been played by multiple&nbsp;
actors across the Star Wars franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:49.620
but when his deformed, weary face is&nbsp;
revealed in Star Wars: Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:53.940
that's classically trained actor Sebastian&nbsp;
Shaw playing the role. He also portrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.600
Anakin's Force ghost at the end of the&nbsp;
original version of Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:00.780
before being replaced by Hayden&nbsp;
Christensen in subsequent releases.

00:04:00.780 --> 00:04:05.820
Shaw was mostly known as a stage actor outside&nbsp;
of Star Wars fandom. He was a member of the Royal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.820 --> 00:04:10.740
Shakespeare Company, where he often played lead&nbsp;
or featured roles. He was also a Broadway veteran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.740 --> 00:04:14.340
and was well known for taking on parts&nbsp;
in daring and controversial plays.

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.060
Lucas choosing to digitally replace&nbsp;
Shaw with Christensen in Return of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.260
the Jedi sparked some controversy at the&nbsp;
time, but Star Wars fans will still never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:28.020
forget the reveal of Darth Vader's face.&nbsp;
Shaw passed away in 1994 at the age of 89.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:33.180
Jason Wingreen didn't actually appear in the Star&nbsp;
Wars films in person, but his voice brought one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.800
of the saga's most iconic villains to life.&nbsp;
Wingreen was the original voice of Boba Fett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:42.420
the intergalactic bounty hunter who tracks&nbsp;
down Han Solo during The Empire Strikes Back.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.900
Wingreen was a well-known television&nbsp;
actor when he auditioned for Star Wars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.800
having appeared in a number of different&nbsp;
shows throughout the '60s, '70s and '80s.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:54.540
He originally auditioned for the role of Yoda,&nbsp;
but lost out on the role and was cast as Boba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:59.220
Fett instead — and only recorded four lines&nbsp;
for the final film. He's not even credited,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.600
and it wasn't until the year 2000 that the public&nbsp;
discovered who had voiced the villain. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:07.200
it's a testament to Wingreen's talent&nbsp;
that Boba Fett became such a massively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:12.840
popular character nonetheless. Wingreen&nbsp;
passed away in 2016 at the age of 95.

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:17.580
You may never have seen Kenny Baker's face&nbsp;
or heard his voice in a Star Wars film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:21.720
but it's a fair bet you still know him —&nbsp;
for most of the movies in the franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:26.100
he portrayed one of sci-fi's&nbsp;
most iconic characters: R2-D2.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:30.900
R2-D2 wasn't a puppet or an animatronic like so&nbsp;
many other creatures in the Star Wars universe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:35.520
Instead, there was an actor inside that&nbsp;
droid. Baker was only 3 foot 8 inches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:39.720
so he could fit inside the chassis used for&nbsp;
filming, and he's the one responsible for crafting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:46.440
much of R2-D2's "personality." When Baker passed&nbsp;
away in 2016 at the age of 81, StarWars.com wrote:

00:05:46.440 --> 00:05:50.100
"Whether it was the slow turn of&nbsp;
Artoo's dome to convey suspicion or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:54.480
nervous wobbles signifying fear, Baker&nbsp;
made a robotic being seem very human."

00:05:54.480 --> 00:05:57.720
While Baker played the droid in&nbsp;
all three original films and two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.720
prequels, he also played an Ewok&nbsp;
[EE-wok] in Return of the Jedi.

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:05.400
Peter Cushing was a massive star when&nbsp;
he was cast as Grand Moff Tarkin in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:08.460
A New Hope. He also brought an&nbsp;
immediate gravitas to the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:13.080
oozing menace and calm and somehow coming off&nbsp;
every bit as intimidating as Darth Vader himself.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:16.620
"Charming, to the last."

00:06:17.520 --> 00:06:22.380
Before appearing in A New Hope, Cushing was an&nbsp;
icon in horror cinema. He appeared in many of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.380 --> 00:06:27.360
the Dracula and Frankenstein films of the '50s and&nbsp;
'60s, and also portrayed Sherlock Holmes in a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:33.000
movie adaptations. After a long and illustrious&nbsp;
career, Cushing passed away in 1994 at the age of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:38.460
81. Despite this, however, he technically reprised&nbsp;
his role as Tarkin in Rogue One: A Star Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:43.500
Story, even though the film came out over 20 years&nbsp;
after his death. Cushing was recreated in the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:48.660
with state of the art CGI, nearly climbing out&nbsp;
of the uncanny valley in the process. Nearly.

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:54.180
Even though Obi-Wan Kenobi died in A New Hope,&nbsp;
Alec Guiness appeared in the other films of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:59.040
original trilogy, thanks to the power of the&nbsp;
Force. His casting lent some credibility to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:03.900
original film, but he also infamously viewed the&nbsp;
story as — in his words — "fairy tale rubbish."

00:07:03.900 --> 00:07:09.000
Guinness was a hugely famous actor when he&nbsp;
signed on for George Lucas' space opera. He'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.320
won an Oscar for The Bridge on the River Kwai&nbsp;
and had starred in classics such as Lawrence of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.180
Arabia and Doctor Zhivago. And while many of the&nbsp;
leading actors in Star Wars are big names now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:23.640
they weren't at the time, making Guinness by far&nbsp;
the biggest star in the movie in 1977. But why did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:28.080
he join the project if he hated the material?&nbsp;
Well, there were a couple of reasons. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:31.920
he thought the moral of the story was&nbsp;
admirable enough. Second, he wouldn't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:37.080
to do any publicity for the film. And third, the&nbsp;
studio offered him a massive salary. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.100
he ignored any fan mail he received about&nbsp;
Star Wars, and hated discussing his role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:46.200
as Obi-Wan. Sir Alec Guinness passed&nbsp;
away in the year 2000 at the age of 86.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.240
One of the most prolific actors in the Star&nbsp;
Wars franchise, Christopher Lee's career spans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.240 --> 00:07:56.760
over 250 roles over the length of his career.&nbsp;
Lee is absolutely one of the gods of geekdom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.960
and has graced several of Hollywood's most&nbsp;
notable franchises. Here, he portrayed Count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.960 --> 00:08:06.060
Dooku in two of the prequel films, Attack of&nbsp;
the Clones and Revenge of the Sith. Lee became&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.860
a star due to his many appearances in the Hammer&nbsp;
Horror Dracula franchise, where, funnily enough,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:16.140
he frequently collaborated with another Star Wars&nbsp;
alum: Peter Cushing. Lee's imposing figure and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:19.980
commanding voice made him perfectly suited&nbsp;
to playing the villain, which helped him a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:24.720
great deal in arguably his biggest ever film&nbsp;
role: playing Saruman in The Lord of the Rings.

00:08:24.720 --> 00:08:28.860
A man of many talents, Christopher Lee&nbsp;
also released a number of heavy metal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.860 --> 00:08:33.720
albums when he was in his 80s and 90s. He was&nbsp;
also knighted, served in the Second World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.720 --> 00:08:38.100
as a member of the RAF and was attached to&nbsp;
the British Army's Long Range Desert Group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.100 --> 00:08:43.920
the precursor of the modern-day SAS. He&nbsp;
passed away in 2015 at the age of 93.

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:49.920
Despite never having showed his face on-screen in&nbsp;
Star Wars, Peter Mayhew quickly became one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:54.420
saga's most iconic characters. His performance&nbsp;
as Chewbacca helped bring a sense of real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:58.680
humanity to a character where many other actors&nbsp;
could easily have been lost beneath the mask.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.380
"Laugh it up, fuzzball."

00:09:04.380 --> 00:09:07.920
Mayhew was a hospital orderly&nbsp;
when he auditioned for Chewbacca,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:13.140
and even kept that job until after Return of the&nbsp;
Jedi was released. The 7 foot 3 inch actor then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.140 --> 00:09:17.220
essentially made his living off the role for&nbsp;
the rest of his life, attending conventions as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:22.740
a fan favorite star for decades. He eventually&nbsp;
reprised the role in 2015's The Force Awakens.

00:09:22.740 --> 00:09:26.760
Nearly everyone who worked with Mayhew on&nbsp;
the Star Wars franchise had nothing but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:31.860
kind things to say about him. After his death&nbsp;
in April 2019, Mark Hamill wrote on Twitter:

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:36.000
"He was the gentlest of giants. A&nbsp;
big man with an even bigger heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:40.320
who never failed to make me smile, and&nbsp;
a loyal friend who I loved dearly. I'm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:44.280
grateful for the memories we shared and I'm&nbsp;
a better man for just having known him."

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:48.300
Peter Diamond was more of a stuntman&nbsp;
and a coordinator than an actor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:53.700
with almost 250 roles in the "stunts" category on&nbsp;
his IMDB page. However, he had a variety of small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.700 --> 00:09:58.020
roles in the original Star Wars trilogy, and he&nbsp;
had a large impact on several memorable scenes.

00:09:58.020 --> 00:10:02.100
Some of the Star Wars roles Diamond took&nbsp;
on include A'Koba, the Tusken Raider who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.100 --> 00:10:06.480
attacks Luke Skywalker, and Garouf Lafoe,&nbsp;
the guy who rats out Luke and Obi-Wan to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:10.620
stormtroopers at the Mos Eisley cantina.&nbsp;
He also appeared as various helmeted or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:14.220
background characters in the trilogy, often&nbsp;
when those roles called for dangerous stunts.

00:10:14.220 --> 00:10:17.880
His other contribution to the original&nbsp;
trilogy came from his role as the stunt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:21.480
coordinator. He actually created two&nbsp;
different styles of lightsaber combat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.480 --> 00:10:26.940
used by the films' Sith and Jedi. Diamond&nbsp;
passed away in March 2004, at the age of 74.

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:32.160
According to the credits of The Empire Strikes&nbsp;
Back, John Hollis played "Lando's Aide," as he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:36.900
not actually given a name in the film. However,&nbsp;
many Star Wars fans know him as Lobot, the cyborg&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.900 --> 00:10:41.220
who helps Lando Calrissian fight back against the&nbsp;
stormtroopers so the heroes can escape Cloud City.

00:10:41.220 --> 00:10:45.660
According to some deeper Star Wars lore, Lobot&nbsp;
originally used his cybernetic enhancements&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:49.440
to augment his intelligence. However,&nbsp;
during a mission gone wrong with Lando,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.440 --> 00:10:53.280
Lobot was injured and lost the aspects&nbsp;
of his mind that made him human. So now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:57.240
he's essentially a computer operating&nbsp;
a human body. His unique cybernetics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.240 --> 00:11:00.120
allow him to have a direct connection&nbsp;
with Cloud City's computer system.

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.700
Hollis appeared in several other popular films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.540
including most of the Christopher Reeves&nbsp;
Superman movies, Flash Gordon, and For Your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.540 --> 00:11:11.880
Eyes Only. After a 40-year acting career,&nbsp;
Hollis passed away in 2005 at 77 years old.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:16.320
In A New Hope, Jek Porkins was a&nbsp;
part of Luke's Red Squadron during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:19.740
the assault on the Death Star. He&nbsp;
flew as Red 6, and unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:23.520
he couldn't contribute much to the battle.&nbsp;
His ship malfunctioned early in the fight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:27.000
and unable to maneuver, he was&nbsp;
quickly shot down by Imperial forces.

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:31.980
Besides his role as Porkins in A New Hope, William&nbsp;
Hootkins also had roles in movies like Flash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:36.960
Gordon, Raiders of the Lost Ark, and Superman IV.&nbsp;
Outside of Star Wars, his most well-known role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:42.000
probably came in 1989's Batman, where he played&nbsp;
corrupt Gotham Police Lieutenant Max Eckhardt. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:46.380
was also a prolific voice actor, as he frequently&nbsp;
lent his voice to radio plays and audio books.

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:50.940
Hootkins died from pancreatic&nbsp;
cancer in 2005. He was 57 years old.

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:56.160
Alex McCrindle played a very small role in&nbsp;
A New Hope, but he was actually the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:00.780
actor to speak one of Star Wars' most famous&nbsp;
quotes. McCrindle played General Dodonna,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.780 --> 00:12:04.800
who tells the Rebels their plan of attack when the&nbsp;
Death Star is approaching. After explaining that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.800 --> 00:12:09.360
the Death Star's only weak point is a tiny exhaust&nbsp;
port, some of the Rebel pilots express concern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.360 --> 00:12:14.040
Luke Skywalker speaks up that the target isn't&nbsp;
impossible, which prompts this line from Dodonna:

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:17.820
"Then man your ships, and&nbsp;
may the Force be with you."

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:21.720
The quote is often mistakenly&nbsp;
attributed to Obi-Wan Kenobi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.720 --> 00:12:23.820
who never actually says that line in A New Hope.

00:12:23.820 --> 00:12:26.580
Alex McCrindle was known more&nbsp;
for television than film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:32.700
and A New Hope is one of the only big name films&nbsp;
to his credit. He died in 1990 at the age of 78.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:36.060
Jack Purvis was one of Hollywood's&nbsp;
most well-known little person actors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:40.320
so it's no surprise that George Lucas cast him in&nbsp;
more significant alien roles across his original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:44.700
trilogy. Purvis actually played three different&nbsp;
characters in the three original Star Wars films.

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:48.840
He appeared as the head Jawa in A New Hope,&nbsp;
the chief Ugnaught in The Empire Strikes Back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.840 --> 00:12:53.160
and Teebo the Ewok in Return of the Jedi. He&nbsp;
was no stranger to the entertainment scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:56.820
as Purvis had been in a touring musical&nbsp;
group with fellow Star Wars actor Kenny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.820 --> 00:13:00.420
Baker. The duo traveled to many bars and&nbsp;
clubs with their musical-comedy show.

00:13:00.420 --> 00:13:04.680
Purvis also appeared in several other&nbsp;
memorable films, mostly in the 1980s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:10.440
such as Time Bandits, Willow, Brazil, and&nbsp;
Labyrinth. Purvis died at the age of 60 in 1997.

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:15.300
Larry Ward might not be the most famous Star&nbsp;
Wars actor with only two movies to his credit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:19.740
A New Hope and Return of the Jedi, but he loaned&nbsp;
his voice to two of Star Wars' most memorable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.740 --> 00:13:24.060
baddies. In the original film, he played the&nbsp;
voice of the notorious bounty hunter, Greedo.

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:30.309
"Jabba wanin cheeco. Wa rush anye&nbsp;
katanye wanaruska, heh heh heh."

00:13:30.309 --> 00:13:31.740
And in Return of the Jedi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:35.580
he provided the dialogue for everybody's&nbsp;
favorite space gangster, Jabba the Hutt.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:39.660
Even more important than giving the giant slug&nbsp;
his voice was helping to develop the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.660 --> 00:13:44.400
language he spoke. The foundation for Huttese&nbsp;
was laid down by sound designer Ben Burtt. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:48.300
based the language on Quechua, the language&nbsp;
of a group of indigenous Peruvians. Burtt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:52.560
played Quechua recordings for Ward, then told the&nbsp;
actor to improvise dialogue that sounded like it.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:55.920
Ward passed away in 2007 at the age of 63.

00:13:56.520 --> 00:14:00.660
Andrew Jack contributed to the Star Wars&nbsp;
franchise in multiple ways. Fans might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:05.520
recognize him as Caluan Ematt, a major in the&nbsp;
Resistance forces in The Force Awakens and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:10.260
general in The Last Jedi. He was also the voice&nbsp;
of Moloch in Solo: A Star Wars Story. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.260 --> 00:14:13.320
Jack's real contribution to Star&nbsp;
Wars came as a dialect coach.

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:18.720
Jack helped many of the actors in the later Star&nbsp;
Wars films perfect their accents. As TMZ noted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:22.020
it's very likely that Jack taught John&nbsp;
Boyega how to hide his British accent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:24.840
for the part of Finn. He also&nbsp;
helped the actors learn to speak&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.840 --> 00:14:28.680
fictional languages. As original Solo&nbsp;
director Christopher Miller explained

00:14:28.680 --> 00:14:32.700
"We asked him to teach Alden [Ehrenreich]&nbsp;
to speak Shyriiwook. I'll always remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.700 --> 00:14:35.460
listening to them gargle-roar&nbsp;
at each other back and forth."

00:14:36.060 --> 00:14:42.156
"Yeah, I speak a little, now just&nbsp;
listen to me you (Chewbacca noises)."

00:14:42.156 --> 00:14:43.500
"(Chewbacca noises)"

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:47.820
Outside of the Star Wars franchise, Jack worked&nbsp;
on accents in some of the biggest films ever made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:50.700
including the Avengers franchise&nbsp;
and the Lord of the Rings series.

00:14:50.700 --> 00:14:55.440
In March 2020, Andrew Jack passed away&nbsp;
from complications due to COVID-19.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:15:00.840
Richard LeParmentier played Admiral Motti,&nbsp;
the man who dared mock Vader's Force practices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:04.860
during a meeting between several Imperial&nbsp;
officers in A New Hope. Confident that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:08.520
Death Star will wipe the Rebels from the&nbsp;
history books, Motti dismisses Vader when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.520 --> 00:15:12.600
the Sith Lord compares the Death Star's&nbsp;
power unfavorably to that of the Force.

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:18.000
"Your sad devotion to that ancient religion has&nbsp;
not helped you conjure up the stolen data tapes."

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:22.320
That's what causes Vader to demonstrate&nbsp;
the Force-choke technique. Motti later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:26.340
directs the Death Star toward Alderaan and&nbsp;
leads Imperial operatives in blowing it up.

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:30.480
LeParmentier relished the role, once&nbsp;
proudly describing Motti as "the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:34.320
Imperial officer who ever stood up to&nbsp;
Darth Vader." The actor even started up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:38.640
a now-defunct Motti fan site and reprised&nbsp;
the character in a 2012 Xbox commercial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:38.640 --> 00:15:43.320
that recreates his most famous scene. Had&nbsp;
the 66-year-old not passed away in 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:47.220
he and other Star Wars actors would've&nbsp;
played their roles once again in Motti Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.220 --> 00:15:51.960
a fan project by LeParmentier that went through&nbsp;
various permutations, including a comic book.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:56.580
Things might've gone better for the Empire if&nbsp;
General Tagge had been involved in overseeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.580 --> 00:16:00.540
the Death Star, rather than Darth Vader and&nbsp;
Grand Moff Tarkin. Played by Don Henderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.540 --> 00:16:04.320
Tagge is in the scene where Vader chokes&nbsp;
Motti, cautioning his fellow Imperial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:08.640
officers to take the Rebels' theft of the&nbsp;
Death Star schematics seriously. Tagge also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:08.640 --> 00:16:12.480
expresses dismay when Tarkin announces the&nbsp;
Imperial Senate's dissolution, but neither&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:16.740
Motti nor Tarkin pay much heed to the heaping&nbsp;
helping of common sense Tagge flings at them.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:19.920
While Star Wars gained Henderson&nbsp;
recognition outside of the U.K.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:19.920 --> 00:16:23.820
Henderson himself admitted to Star Wars: The&nbsp;
Official Magazine that his involvement had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:23.820 --> 00:16:27.720
slipped his mind until he saw it with his son&nbsp;
in the movie theater. That didn't stop him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:27.720 --> 00:16:32.280
having a healthy acting career that lasted nearly&nbsp;
three decades, particularly gaining acclaim and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:37.260
attention in the U.K. for portraying quirky law&nbsp;
enforcement officer George Bulman in The XYY Man.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.260 --> 00:16:44.040
His career also involved a notable role in Doctor&nbsp;
Who. Henderson died on June 22nd, 1997, just a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.040 --> 00:16:47.760
months after his character returned to theaters&nbsp;
in the Special Edition version of A New Hope.

00:16:48.360 --> 00:16:52.200
Though not a major character, you'll likely&nbsp;
remember Eddie Byrne's Commander Willard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:56.100
the first fellow member of the Rebellion&nbsp;
whom Leia interacts with on Yavin IV when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:59.880
she finally arrives. Though a small part,&nbsp;
Willard actually does do something fairly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.880 --> 00:17:04.020
important in the film; namely having the&nbsp;
Death Star plans extracted from R2-D2 so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.020 --> 00:17:06.240
the Rebels can figure out how to&nbsp;
attack the moon-shaped station.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:12.240
Byrne was no stranger to two of the higher-profile&nbsp;
actors in A New Hope. In 1966's Island of Terror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.240 --> 00:17:16.980
Byrne, portraying Dr. Reginald Landers, seeks&nbsp;
out help from pathologist Brian Stanley,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.980 --> 00:17:21.840
played by Grand Moff Tarkin actor Peter&nbsp;
Cushing. And in the 1959 movie The Scapegoat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.840 --> 00:17:25.500
Byrne plays a friendly bartender who helps&nbsp;
Sir Alec Guinness' character become better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.500 --> 00:17:30.180
acquainted with his physical double, also played&nbsp;
by Guinness. Sadly, the chances of the three&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:35.640
actors appearing in another film together ended&nbsp;
when Eddie Byrne passed away on August 21st, 1981.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:41.280
Given how many characters in Star Wars wear armor&nbsp;
or are aliens, there are plenty of actors from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:46.020
saga whose faces you never see. Such is the case&nbsp;
with Des Webb, who is the Wampa that Luke and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:50.520
his tauntaun run into while making the rounds on&nbsp;
Hoth. While Webb himself isn't visible to viewers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:55.080
as he's inside a big hairy costume, fans of the&nbsp;
unaltered original trilogy may recall that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.080 --> 00:18:00.000
Wampa doesn't appear on-screen quite as much&nbsp;
before 1997. Blame Webb's costume, which was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:03.900
just too cumbersome for the actor to pull off&nbsp;
all the Wampa's feats. The Special Edition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.900 --> 00:18:08.280
version of Empire added more Wampa footage,&nbsp;
with Howie Weed stepping into a new costume.

00:18:08.280 --> 00:18:11.880
Webb's acting roles were few and&nbsp;
low-key. Interestingly, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:16.320
the final film he's credited in, Morons from&nbsp;
Outer Space, has an intro that emulates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.320 --> 00:18:20.760
scene in A New Hope where Darth Vader's Star&nbsp;
Destroyer first appears. Webb died on May 21st,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.760 --> 00:18:24.780
2002, five days after Attack of&nbsp;
the Clones made its U.S. debut.

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:29.820
The man in the battered Mandalorian armor in&nbsp;
both Empire and Return of the Jedi is Jeremy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.820 --> 00:18:34.440
Bulloch. As Bulloch explained to Sci-Fi Central,&nbsp;
he was actually juggling his days shooting as Boba&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:38.700
Fett with his evening job as a theater actor. The&nbsp;
packed schedule didn't affect his enthusiasm for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:38.700 --> 00:18:42.780
the role, though he admitted to StarWars.com&nbsp;
that the costume wasn't exactly the coziest.

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:46.440
Bulloch being the original Boba Fett is&nbsp;
well-known to the Star Wars faithful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:50.520
but perhaps not as common knowledge is the fact&nbsp;
that Bullock appears unmasked in Revenge of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:50.520 --> 00:18:54.060
Sith as Captain Colton, who takes several&nbsp;
of the heroes to Coruscant to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:58.200
why Clone Troopers are killing Jedi. He's&nbsp;
also Empire lackey Lieutenant Sheckil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:58.200 --> 00:19:01.800
who seizes Leia when she warns Luke that&nbsp;
Vader's been expecting the young Jedi.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:05.880
Bulloch appeared in several features, and in&nbsp;
fan films afterward in roles that paid homage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.880 --> 00:19:11.280
to his iconic character. He passed away from&nbsp;
Parkinson's complications on December 17th, 2020.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:16.560
Irish actor Ray Stevenson first ventured&nbsp;
into a galaxy far, far away in Star Wars:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:20.340
Rebels as the voice of Mandalorian&nbsp;
Gar Saxon, leader of the Mandalorian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:24.180
Super commandos. He later reprised the&nbsp;
role in the Clone Wars animated series.

00:19:24.180 --> 00:19:28.080
Marvel fans might recognize him as&nbsp;
Frank Castle in Punisher: War Zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:31.920
or the MCU's Volstagg. His roles also&nbsp;
extended into other popular series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:31.920 --> 00:19:36.240
on both the big and small screens, like&nbsp;
Dexter, the Divergent series, and Vikings.

00:19:36.240 --> 00:19:40.860
Stevenson's reinsertion into the Star Wars&nbsp;
franchise came posthumously. He passed away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:46.440
on May 21st, 2023, only three months before the&nbsp;
debut of Ahsoka, where he portrays the mysterious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.440 --> 00:19:51.660
Baylan Skoll. While Stevenson is not alive to see&nbsp;
fans' reaction to Ahsoka, he's expressed great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.660 --> 00:19:56.580
joy in taking part, telling Screen Rant Plus he&nbsp;
considers the series "sprinkled with pixie dust."

